// ==WindhawkModReadme==
/*
# Custom Application Icons

Assigns your own icon to a running program. The replacement happens at the
source - inside the program itself - so every consumer of that icon picks it
up: the notification area, the taskbar, the Alt+Tab switcher, the classic
title bar, Task Manager and anything else that asks the window for its icon.

## What it hooks

| Surface | How the icon is obtained | What the mod does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Tray | `Shell_NotifyIcon` called by the program | Swaps `hIcon` in a private copy of the structure before the call reaches the tray |
| Taskbar, Alt+Tab, title bar | `WM_GETICON` sent to the window | Answers the message with the replacement icon |
| Switchers that prefer the file icon | `PrivateExtractIcons` / `ExtractIconEx` / `SHGetFileInfo` on the program's `.exe` | Returns the replacement icon for index 0 |
| Programs Windhawk cannot enter | `SendMessageTimeout(WM_GETICON)` from the taskbar or switcher | Answers on the asking side, so the icon changes even without being inside the program |

Changes take effect immediately after **Save** - no need to restart the
program, the shell or Explorer. Tray icons that were registered before the mod
was loaded are refreshed by asking their owner to register them again, the
same `TaskbarCreated` handshake the shell uses after an Explorer restart.

## Icon syntax

* `C:\Icons\steam.ico` - an icon file, the best matching frame is used
* `C:\Program Files\App\app.exe,2` - icon number 2 inside a file
* `%SystemRoot%\System32\shell32.dll,-16` - a negative number is a resource ID

Environment variables are expanded.

## Matching

**Program** accepts an executable name (`steam.exe`) or a full path
(`C:\Program Files*\steam.exe`); `*` and `?` wildcards are allowed and case is
ignored. Several programs can share one rule when separated by `|`, which is
what a modern app usually needs - the window you see on the taskbar often
belongs to a helper process, not to the executable you started:
`steam.exe|steamwebhelper.exe`. Task Manager, "Go to details", tells you which
process owns a window.

**Window class** and **Window title** are optional extra conditions, used to
give different windows of the same program different icons. They are ignored
for tray icons, which are matched by program only.

## A different icon for each state

A program with several states does not name them - it simply hands over a
different icon. That icon is the identity, so the mod fingerprints its pixels
and a rule can ask for one fingerprint.

1. Add a rule naming the program and leave its icon empty: the program is now
   watched without anything being changed.
2. Turn on **Record the icons programs hand out** and use the program until it
   has been through the states you care about.
3. Open the capture folder. Every distinct icon is there as
   `<fingerprint>_<size>.ico`, and `captured.txt` lists where each came from -
   tray or window - with the tooltip or title at the time.
4. Put the fingerprint of the state you want to change into **Icon being
   replaced**, and your own icon next to it. Repeat for the other states.
5. Turn recording off.

Two shapes of program come out of this:

* **A fixed set of state icons** - Discord's normal, unread and muted logos are
  three drawings, so three rules cover them exactly.
* **An icon drawn on the fly** - Telegram paints the unread number into its
  icon, so every count is its own fingerprint and pinning them all is hopeless.
  Pin the one plain state instead, and let a second rule with no conditions
  catch everything else: the first rule is "no mail", the second "some mail".
  Rules are tried in order, so the specific one has to come first.

For the taskbar and the switcher there is a second lever: the unread count
usually appears in the window title as well, so **Window title** with `* – (*)`
tells the states apart without any fingerprint at all.

While a state rule exists, the mod stops assigning icons to windows and only
answers requests - writing our icon into the window would erase the very thing
the states are told apart by.

## Known limits

* Store/UWP apps take their icon from their package, not from the window, so
  they cannot be redirected this way.
* Windhawk skips some processes entirely - sandboxed programs, and everything a
  game launcher starts (`Inject into games` is off by default, which is why
  `steamwebhelper.exe`, the process that owns Steam's window, is never entered).
  Their taskbar and switcher icons are still replaced, from the asking side,
  but their own title bar and tray icon are out of reach.
* Consumers that read the icon out of the system image list
  (`SHGFI_SYSICONINDEX` - what RetroBar falls back to for a window with no icon
  at all) are not affected; use `icon-resource-redirect` for those.
* A consumer that caches the icon handle it received keeps showing the old
  icon until it re-reads it. Windows are poked after every settings change, so
  this only shows up in switchers with their own per-executable cache.
*/
// ==/WindhawkModReadme==

